# Rank 1789 / CVE-2025-61600: the IMAP/ManageSieve command receiver
# parsed quoted arguments one byte at a time, and every arm of the
# quoted-argument state that appends to the accumulator carried the
# 4096-byte bound except the escaped-backslash arm -- `b'\\' => { if
# escaped { self.buf.push(ch); } ... }` pushed with no comparison at
# all, so a peer sending a quoted argument made of escaped
# backslashes grew the buffer past the cap without limit
# (unbounded per-connection accumulation, CWE-400; fix commit
# a8e631e88 "IMAP: Check buf size before pushing escape sequences
# within quoted literals"). The fixed revision wraps that one push in the same
# `self.buf.len() < QUOTED_ARG_MAX_LEN` test its sibling arms
# carry, lifting the literal into a named constant; the
# sibling arms' bounds and the rest of the state machine are
# unchanged, which is what makes the defect an arm-local omission
# rather than a missing global cap.
#
# ATTENTION, engine gap: branch regions inside a Rust loop. The
# discriminating fact is per-arm -- "this append is dominated by a
# size comparison" -- and the engine has the machinery for
# exactly that relation (`unless X.dominates coveredBy <check>` over
# the structured control regions). It is unusable here because the
# Rust frontend lowers no loop at all: `while let`/`while`/`for`/
# `loop` have no case in `rsConv.stmtH` (internal/extract/frontend/
# treesitter/rust.go:126) nor in `expr`, so the whole loop falls
# through to the catch-all `nir.Seq` and every node inside lands in
# one flat function region. With flat regions `Dominates` degenerates
# to source order, so the bound sitting in the `b'"'` arm three arms
# away "dominates" the unguarded push and suppresses it. Measured
# with a throwaway pair of bindings (a `push` call labelled as an
# unbounded-accumulation issue, a `len` call labelled as the size
# check, joined by `unless p.dominates coveredBy`): on the identical
# arm structure with the match in statement position the rule
# reports exactly one finding, on the unguarded push, and nothing on
# the form whose arm carries its own bound; the same two functions
# inside a `while let` loop report nothing at either revision. On the
# real tree (crates/imap-proto scanned with --cache off, three runs
# per revision) the shipped definitions report 7 findings at the
# parent and 7 at the fixed revision, all VYQL-MEM-006 NullableDeref noise, none
# on receiver.rs. No definition change shipped: a rule emitted today
# would fire only where a function carries no bound of its own -- the
# 78 of 797 Rust files in this tree with a loop, a push and a length
# comparison are the precision surface, and the inconsistent-bound
# signal that narrows them is the half the engine cannot currently
# express. ENGINE-GAPS.md entry filed for the loop lowering; the
# binding and rule are the data half that follows it.
#
# Spec cve_rank1789 records the unbounded arm and the bounded arm,
# both asserting reject VYQL-DOS-042: when the loop lowering lands
# branch regions and an unbounded-accumulation binding ships, cases
# one and three must flip to expect.

test "rank1789: the escaped-backslash arm appends without the bound its siblings carry"
  lang rust
  file crates/imap-proto/src/receiver.rs
  reject VYQL-DOS-042
  code
  ```
  const QUOTED_ARG_MAX_LEN: usize = 4096;

  enum State {
      Argument { last_ch: u8 },
      ArgumentQuoted { escaped: bool },
  }

  struct Receiver {
      buf: Vec<u8>,
      state: State,
  }

  impl Receiver {
      fn error_reset(&mut self, _: &str) -> Error {
          self.buf.clear();
          Error::TooLong
      }

      pub fn parse(&mut self, bytes: &mut std::slice::Iter<'_, u8>) -> Result<(), Error> {
          #[allow(clippy::while_let_on_iterator)]
          while let Some(&ch) = bytes.next() {
              match self.state.clone() {
                  State::Argument { last_ch } => match ch {
                      b'"' if last_ch.is_ascii_whitespace() => {
                          self.state = State::ArgumentQuoted { escaped: false };
                      }
                      _ => {
                          if self.buf.len() < QUOTED_ARG_MAX_LEN {
                              self.buf.push(ch);
                              self.state = State::Argument { last_ch: ch };
                          } else {
                              return Err(self.error_reset("Quoted argument too long."));
                          }
                      }
                  },
                  State::ArgumentQuoted { escaped } => match ch {
                      b'"' => {
                          if !escaped {
                              self.buf.clear();
                              self.state = State::Argument { last_ch: b' ' };
                          } else if self.buf.len() < QUOTED_ARG_MAX_LEN {
                              self.buf.push(ch);
                              self.state = State::ArgumentQuoted { escaped: false };
                          } else {
                              return Err(self.error_reset("Quoted argument too long."));
                          }
                      }
                      b'\\' => {
                          if escaped {
                              self.buf.push(ch);
                          }
                          self.state = State::ArgumentQuoted { escaped: !escaped };
                      }
                      b'\n' => {
                          return Err(self.error_reset("Unterminated quoted argument."));
                      }
                      _ => {
                          if self.buf.len() < QUOTED_ARG_MAX_LEN {
                              if escaped {
                                  self.buf.push(b'\\');
                              }
                              self.buf.push(ch);
                              self.state = State::ArgumentQuoted { escaped: false };
                          } else {
                              return Err(self.error_reset("Quoted argument too long."));
                          }
                      }
                  },
              }
          }
          Ok(())
      }
  }

  enum Error {
      TooLong,
  }
  ```

